oyable to use  day to day now when the m1 macbooks came  out suddenly we had this unbelievable  change where even the fanless m1 macbook  air performed astonishingly well and  could handle any work that most people  would throw at it and sure the m1 cpus  had absolutely insane performance but  for those that needed extra graphics  performance this was the weak spot for  the machine and it could get  significantly slower if you do that kind  of work now because of that if i needed  great total performance i still have to  go back to a 16-inch macbook pro despite  so many drawbacks like way worse battery  life loud fan noise and much bigger size  that is where this current paradigm  shift came in and for the first time  ever in macbook history you could spec  the new 14-inch macbook pro exactly the  same as the larger 16 inch size with up  to 64 gigabytes of ram an m1 max 32 core  processor and 8 terabytes of ssd space  but with that exciting change we also  had two changes that made this seemingly  easy choice much more difficult and that  put me and many of you guys back to  square one and i was sitting there  contemplating how to make the correct  choice because of five key points now if  you guys watched apple's keynote it  seemed like you just choose the size you  want but at max tech we found some major  differences the first one was battery  life now one of the big reasons in the  past to get a 13 inch was the better  battery life but with these new 14 inch  and 16 inch apple silicon machines the  16 inch gets significantly better  battery life with the 14 inch barely  improving over the past 13 inch machines  while the 16 has insane battery life and  of course performance both for simple  tests and heavy loads the second was of  course the screen size with that size  still being preferable for productivity  tasks and the third was fan noise with  the 16 inch running cooler and quieter  than the 14 inch and then we have actual  real world performance and the fa